 {"id": "DF-OFF-BY-ONE-29", "title": "Idle gate is disabled (-load-threshold -1): the solver runs continuously even when the host is saturated", "status": "complete", "priority": "P0", "complexity": 2, "reasoning": "deploy/off-by-one.service:55 ships `-load-threshold -1`, and the binary's own help says \"Max loadavg(1) for idle detection (negative = always idle) (default 1)\". The lab's stated purpose is to convert IDLE cycles into answers, so -1 removes the very gate that keeps it opportunistic. Measured consequence 2026-10-03: with loadavg ~60 and io pressure 95%, the daemon kept dequeueing and launching solves every 60s; an AI-generated Go test (bbr.test) reached 47 GB RSS = 75% of RAM and swap filled to 55.5 GiB of 55 GiB (completely full), TWICE inside twenty minutes. Prometheus on fleet-monitor: node_memory_MemAvailable 44.7 -> 4.1 GiB, node_memory_AnonPages 8.5 -> 49.3 GiB, peak memory pressure some avg10 = 34.9%. Full evidence: ~/reports/ram-incident-2026-10-03/index.html (section 4).", "capability_tags": ["ops", "deploy", "config"], "fix_direction": "Restore a real threshold in deploy/off-by-one.service: replace `-load-threshold -1` with a positive value (default 1; o=4 is a defensible start for a 16-core box that also runs the fleet). The repo file is the source of truth \u2014 the host currently carries an ops drop-in with only memory limits, so a redeploy from deploy/ would reintroduce -1. Acceptance: (a) `grep load-threshold deploy/off-by-one.service` shows a positive number; (b) journal shows a skip/back-off line when loadavg(1) >= the threshold, produced by a repro (run a bounded load generator to push loadavg above the threshold and observe no solve starts); (c) the chosen value and the reason are recorded in the repo (unit comment or docs/).", "worker_status": "complete", "commit_hash": "95a66683", "guard_result": "PASS", "ci_result": "GREEN", "worker_summary": "Foreman-direct: restored -load-threshold 4 in deploy/off-by-one.service. Guard DEGRADED 