 {"id": "DF-OFF-BY-ONE-30", "title": "No memory bound on AI-written code: one generated test reached 47 GB and filled swap; the repo unit must carry the cap", "status": "complete", "priority": "P0", "complexity": 3, "reasoning": "The solver compiles and runs code that an LLM wrote, and nothing in the chain limits its memory: bwrap isolates filesystem/PID/session namespaces but not memory (internal/sandbox/bwrap.go documents exactly that scope), the unit ran MemoryMax=infinity/MemoryHigh=infinity, and the only cap was time (internal/solver/piagent.go: DefaultSolveTimeout = 30 min). On 2026-10-03 a generated test allocated 47 GB in minutes; because swap absorbed it (46 GB, then 55.5 GB = 100% of swap) the kernel never OOM-killed it, so the host thrashed for ~10 minutes instead of failing fast. Ops applied a HOST-ONLY drop-in (/etc/systemd/system/off-by-one.service.d/memory-cap.conf: MemoryHigh=6G, MemoryMax=8G, MemorySwapMax=2G, verified effective) \u2014 but that is not in the repo and will not survive a redeploy.", "capability_tags": ["ops", "deploy", "sandbox", "tooling"], "fix_direction": "Make the bound durable and per-solve: (a) add MemoryHigh/MemoryMax/MemorySwapMax to deploy/off-by-one.service so a redeploy cannot ship an uncapped solver; (b) cap each solve individually (e.g. run the sandbox under `systemd-run --scope -p MemoryMax=<cap>` or set RLIMIT_AS in internal/sandbox config) so one bad problem cannot consume the daemon's whole budget; (c) surface the kill \u2014 a cgroup OOM kill of a generated test must produce a clear log line naming the problem id. Acceptance: a deliberate runaway fixture (test that allocates unboundedly) is killed at the cap with a named log line, the daemon survives it, and host MemAvailable stays above a floor for the whole test; verified by a self-test that runs that fixture and asserts the kill + the log line.", "worker_status": "complete", "commit_hash": "c48b8979", "guard_result": "PASS", "completed_at": "2026-10-04 01:20:00", "updated_at": "2026-10-04 01:20:00", "worker_summary": "Adopted the orphaned 2026-10-03 work (sandbox memcap committed in c1deec5f but the wrapper/flag/unit wiring was uncommitted, worker dead): oby-memcap exec wrapper (1cbf17ca), -solve-mem-mb flag + durable 6G/8G/2G unit caps (c48b8979), and the judge-required kernel-level inheritance test TestObyMemcapExec_ChildInheritsCap (real wrapper exec -> /proc/self/limits soft==hard 67108864 bytes). Judge FAIL 3fa7b03f -> PASS 6b352678. Full suite green; repo-wide golangci-lint 0. Binary rebuilt+redeployed (kill MainPID -> Restart=always, fresh PID 2286235->). NOTE: host unit /etc/systemd/system/off-by-one.service ExecStart still lacks -solve-mem-mb 6144 (approval-gated); the 8G cgroup drop-in caps ARE live. DF-29 (load-threshold 4) landed by a sibling tick (ef7b6b63/fc8b9364).", "foreman_note": "tick off-by-one-foreman-2026-10-04-05-44-08: adopted orphaned in-progress work, judged PASS, deployed."}
 {"id": "DF-OFF-BY-ONE-31", "title": "Generated solution accumulates per-event slices with no bound \u2014 the 47 GB allocation is in the solved code, not the harness", "status": "pending", "priority": "P1", "complexity": 3, "reasoning": "The runaway was not the daemon (live cgroup 0.26 GB after the test exited) \u2014 it was the generated packet-level simulator. In the sandbox for problem class go-bbr-pacing-congestion-bandwidth-delay-probe (problem.json, 11:02) the agent wrote bbr/bbr.go (425 lines), bbr/sim.go (309 lines) and two *_test.go files; sim.go appends once per simulated event and never trims: sim.go:189 `s.queueAt = append(s.queueAt, QueuePoint{...})` and sim.go:106 `s.lossPlan = append(s.lossPlan, lossInjection{...})`. A deep-buffer bottleneck with a scripted ACK trace generates an unbounded number of events, so memory scales with simulated packets. The agent itself added an iteration guard in the test (debug_test.go:30 `if count > 5000000`) \u2014 evidence it already knew the loop could run away. Host-capping this (DF-OFF-BY-ONE-30) stops the blast radius but does not fix a solver that produces unbounded simulations.", "capability_tags": ["solver", "sandbox", "tooling", "ci"], "fix_direction": "Bound the work the solver is allowed to produce, at both ends. Prompt side: require that solutions to simulation classes declare their bounds (max simulated events/packets/seconds) and aggregate incrementally instead of retaining a record per event; state a memory budget the generated tests must respect. Harness side: measure the peak RSS of each test run and treat a run that approaches the cap as a rejected answer (with the measured number recorded against the problem id), so a rejected problem produces a finding rather than a host incident. Acceptance: (a) an unbounded simulation fixture is REJECTED by the harness with its measured peak recorded; (b) a bounded version of the same problem is accepted and verified; (c) the prompt/contract change is asserted by a test so it cannot silently regress."}
